  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "f1",
      "text": "In 1984 there were about 231 grams of rice per person per day for food in Taiwan; by 2025 there were about 114 grams.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "text": "Wheat supplied for food rose from about 64 grams per person per day in 1984 to about 106 grams in 2005, and was about 98 grams in 2025.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s1"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "text": "Rice eaten per person per year in Taiwan fell from 99.4 kg in 1981 to 45.7 kg in 2014.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f4",
      "text": "In 2022 the average person in Taiwan ate 42.98 kg of rice, 0.05 kg less than the year before.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s6"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f5",
      "text": "In 2022 people in Taiwan ate more meat than grains for the first time since the statistics began: 87.5 kg of meat and 87.4 kg of grains per person.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s6"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f6",
      "text": "The farm agency says Western-style diets and more people eating out have changed how rice is eaten, and young people prefer noodles and baked goods.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "cause_effect"
    },
    {
      "id": "f7",
      "text": "An agriculture ministry article links falling rice eating to rising incomes, changes in society and Western food culture.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "cause_effect"
    },
    {
      "id": "f8",
      "text": "Over about 30 years before 2011, rice eaten per person per year fell from 98 kg to 48 kg, while flour rose from 23 kg to 36 kg.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s5"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f9",
      "text": "From the 1950s the United States gave wheat and flour to many developing countries, including Taiwan.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s4"
      ],
      "kind": "event"
    },
    {
      "id": "f10",
      "text": "In the 1960s a campaign promoted wheat foods; newspapers then claimed Westerners were taller because they ate bread.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s4"
      ],
      "kind": "event"
    },
    {
      "id": "f11",
      "text": "Congee made from leftover rice has long appeared on many breakfast tables in Taiwan.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s8"
      ],
      "kind": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": "f12",
      "text": "For people in Taiwan the sandwich is now as typical a breakfast as steamed buns; quick breakfast stands such as the Mei Er Mei chain made it common.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s7"
      ],
      "kind": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": "f13",
      "text": "To help farmers, the government promotes rice flour, made by grinding rice, mixed with wheat flour in foods young people like, such as noodles and bread.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s2"
      ],
      "kind": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": "f14",
      "text": "In the same period that rice eating fell by about half, meat eaten per person per year rose from 43 kg to 72 kg.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s3"
      ],
      "kind": "number"
    },
    {
      "id": "f15",
      "text": "Rice has no gluten, and Western-style baked goods rarely use rice, partly because rice costs more than wheat on world markets.",
      "source_ids": [
        "s4",
        "s5"
      ],
      "kind": "other"
    }
  ],
